in @prism
in @meta
in @io
in @option
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/reality
in @epistemologic/reality/silicon
in @epistemologic/property
in @epistemologic/property/content_addressed

# @epistemologic/reality/silicon/memory
#
# The memory model as a typed carrier. Names what an observer sees
# when they look at the substrate's address space: which memory
# model the silicon presents (UMA, separate host-device, NUMA),
# how much memory is available in total, what the page size is,
# and a summary of the cache hierarchy.
#
# Per docs/specs/shard-design.md §2/§4: this is the carrier the
# shard's `memory` field holds. Per docs/insights/2026-05-25-pipe-
# hole-and-au-binary.md: Fate's |\> tournament uses the memory
# model to pick between UMA-aware codegen (zero-copy host↔device
# transfers on Apple Silicon) and explicit-DMA codegen (cloud GPU
# with PCIe). The model choice is load-bearing for performance and
# verifiable from the running system.
#
# Same tick-1 discipline as the arch carriers: ship the carrier
# now; detect() body is a Fate-resolved hole filled at boot.
#
# Substrate-pull placement (2026-06-06, Mara's reality family migration):
# moved from shards/epistemologic/silicon/ to shards/epistemologic/reality/silicon/
# under the @epistemologic/reality family root.
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/epistemologic/reality/silicon/memory.mirror` declares
# `@epistemologic/reality/silicon/memory` and only that.

prism @epistemologic/reality/silicon/memory {
  focus memory
  project memory
  split memory
  shift memory
  settle memory
}

# Memory model. UMA (Apple Silicon: unified memory, GPU sees the
# same address space as CPU); separate (host CPU + discrete GPU,
# explicit transfers required); NUMA (multi-socket servers with
# per-node memory affinity, the u32 names the node count).
type memory_model = uma | separate | numa(u32)

# Cache level summary. Per-level size in bytes; the carrier ships
# the field set as `option(u64)` because not every level is
# populated on every silicon (some embedded cores have no L3;
# the AMX coprocessor's own L1 is not separately surfaced). Field
# presence reflects what the detection primitive could read.
type cache_level = {
  l1: option(u64),
  l2: option(u64),
  l3: option(u64),
}

# The memory carrier. Four fields. `model` is the structural
# choice; `total_bytes` is the address-space cap; `page_size` is
# the granularity (4K / 16K on Apple Silicon / 64K on some ARM
# servers); `cache` is the hierarchy summary.
#
# Per-field nullability for cache is encoded inside cache_level;
# total_bytes and page_size are always populated by detect() on
# platforms the substrate supports.
type memory = {
  model:       memory_model,
  total_bytes: u64,
  page_size:   u64,
  cache:       cache_level,
}

requires content_addressed(memory)

# Detection primitive. Reads hw.memsize / hw.pagesize / hw.l*cachesize
# on Darwin via @io.exec("sysctl", ...). On Linux: /proc/meminfo for
# total, getconf PAGESIZE for page size, /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/
# cache/index*/size for cache hierarchy. NUMA detection: presence of
# /sys/devices/system/node/ enumerates the nodes. Body is a Fate-
# resolved hole; the tournament picks the right detection chain for
# the running OS at boot.
detect() -> memory { \ }

# Literal property. Same discipline as the arch carriers: re-detect,
# content-address, compare. Memory configuration is stable across
# a kernel session (unless hotplug, which the property does not yet
# account for) so the OID equality holds.
property literal(m: memory) -> verdict { \ }

out @epistemologic/reality/silicon/memory
out memory
out memory_model
out cache_level
out detect
out literal
